      {
        "ballot_id": "B017",
        "laureates": [
          "Michael Berry",
          "Yakir Aharonov"
        ],
        "citation": "For revealing the physical significance of quantum phase through the Aharonov–Bohm effect and the geometric phase."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B003",
    "B019"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "The Aharonov–Bohm effect and Berry phase show that quantum phase carries physical information with observable consequences, extending across many areas of physics. The ideas have been experimentally confirmed and used for decades, making this a mature, foundational contribution. Berry and Aharonov made distinct contributions that can be recognized in one carefully worded citation.",
    "case_against": "These are historically separate developments, not one experiment or a single discovery. A shared award could blur their distinct origins, and the choice of just two names leaves a broad history of experimental and theoretical contributions outside the citation.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig likewise sees deep, lasting significance in the Aharonov–Bohm effect and Berry phase, while noting that they arose in different periods and that the citation needs a precise scope.",
        "response": "I agree on the historical distinction. The case for pairing them is that both established observable consequences of quantum phase, a common physical insight that can be stated without implying they were one discovery. The citation should name both effects explicitly."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson favors topological insulators and quantized edge transport for their far-reaching framework and clear experimental signatures, while noting that the ballot joins distinct milestones.",
        "response": "The material realizations make a strong case, especially because the signatures can be measured directly. I remain more persuaded by B017 because its underlying effects are experimentally established across a long history and have broad conceptual reach. The combined milestones and difficult attribution boundaries in B021 make me less certain about its scope."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the Aharonov–Bohm effect and Berry phase should be joined in one award or treated as separate contributions requires careful definition of the prize scope."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
